{{smaller block|Challenges are of two kinds; first, to the ''array'', when exception is taken to the whole number impaneled; and secondly, to the polls, when individual jurymen are objected to. ''A. Fonblanque, Jr.'', How we are Governed, xvii.}}
'''8.''' Formerly, in England, the muster of a county for military purposes; the men so mustered: as, a commission of ''array''. See ''commission''.
{{smaller block|Y<sup>e</sup> Parliament had extreamely worried him for attempting to put in execution y<sup>e</sup> commission of ''aray'', and for which the rest of his collegues were hanged by y<sup>e</sup> rebells.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, March 23, 1646.}}
Previous to the reign of Henry VIII., in order to protect the kingdom from domestic insurrections or the prospects of foreign invasions, it was usual from time to time for our princes to issue commissions of ''array''. ''Wharton.''}}
'''9.''' In ''math.'', a collection of quantities arranged in a rectangular block; a matrix.—'''Challenge to the array.''' {{smaller|See ''challenge''.}}

'''arrayal''' (a-rā′a̤l), ''n.'' [< ''array'' + ''-al''.] The process of arraying; muster of a force; array. ''N. E. D.''

